// Retract/assert atomicity (SPEC.SCRATCH.md SS7, SS10 "Verification
// strategy", SS15's first named candidate for a real Thermite contract).
//
// SPEC-DRIVEN, DELIBERATELY IN THAT ORDER: this contract (signature +
// req/ens/fx) was written and checked into forge's goal state BEFORE any
// implementation existed -- the body started as a hole (?0), filled only
// after `forge goal` reported the given/want. See dev-journal for the
// full session, including a struct-wrapped first draft that turned out
// to hit a real Thermite limitation (below).
//
// Claim under test: a combined commit's retract-half and assert-half apply
// together or not at all -- never partially. Modeled as: retracted-fact
// records and newly-asserted-fact records each live in their own
// presence map (Map<u64, bool>, keyed by a fact id). "Retract" and
// "assert" are each modeled as additive inserts, never an in-place
// mutation or removal -- this isn't a modeling convenience, it's the same
// append-only principle this project's own design already commits to
// (SPEC.md SS3/SS7: retraction is always additive, never destructive), so
// the model and the real design agree on this by construction, not
// coincidence.
//
// The req guards (both keys absent beforehand, both maps under Map's
// capacity bound, and the two keys distinct) rule out a vacuous contract.
// The ens pins BOTH resulting presence facts AND the exact size growth of
// both maps in the SAME returned pair, so there is no return path that
// performs only one half: partial application (retract without assert,
// or vice versa) is a contract violation Forge can actually catch, not
// just an intention.
//
// KNOWN THERMITE LIMITATION, found by testing, not assumed: the natural
// first draft of this modeled retracted/asserted as two fields of one
// `struct WorldState { retracted: Map<u64,bool>, asserted: Map<u64,bool> }`
// -- that version parses and WorldState itself certifies L3, but calling
// `.insert()` on a Map field reached through a struct (even after moving
// the field out to a local first) fails to discharge insert's own
// precondition, while the identical insert on a bare Map *parameter*
// (never wrapped in a struct) discharges cleanly -- isolated against a
// same-shaped Vec-in-struct probe, which certifies fine, so this is
// specific to Map-in-struct, not composite-types-in-general. Worked
// around below by keeping both maps as separate parameters/return values
// instead of struct fields. Not filed upstream (this session has
// read-only access to Corvidae-Coding-Projects/Thermite); worth a real
// issue there if this project adopts Thermite further.

fn apply_combined_commit(
  retracted: Map<u64, bool>,
  asserted: Map<u64, bool>,
  retract_key: u64,
  assert_key: u64
) -> (Map<u64, bool>, Map<u64, bool>)
  req !retracted.contains_key(retract_key) && retracted.len() < 1_000_000
      && !asserted.contains_key(assert_key) && asserted.len() < 1_000_000
      && retract_key != assert_key
  ens result.0.contains_key(retract_key)
  ens result.1.contains_key(assert_key)
  ens result.0.len() == retracted.len() + 1
  ens result.1.len() == asserted.len() + 1
  fx  alloc
{
  let mut retracted2 = retracted;
  let mut asserted2 = asserted;
  retracted2.insert(retract_key, true);
  asserted2.insert(assert_key, true);
  (retracted2, asserted2)
}
